    "evidence_text": "Statin prophylaxis and inflammatory mediators following cardiopulmonary bypass: a systematic review\n\nDescriptive analysis was used when data could not be pooled.   \n\n\nSETTING Data from 510 different hospitals across the United States participating in the Premier Perspective database.   \n\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURES Acute renal failure and thromboembolic, cardiac, and pulmonary complications.   \n\n\nA recent decrease in hydroxyethyl starch 6% use was noted, whereas that of albumin 5% increased.   \n\n\nThis increased risk also applied to albumin 5%.\n\nThese findings raise questions regarding the widespread use of these colloids in elective joint arthroplasty procedures.\n\nPreoperative statin treatment reduces systemic inflammatory response and myocardial damage in cardiac surgery.\n\nRESULTS The baseline, operative and postoperative morbidity and mortality characteristics were similar for both the groups.\n\nThe impact of hydroxyethyl starches in cardiac surgery: a meta-analysis\n\nIt remains unclear whether this also applies to patient populations that are less vulnerable.\n\nSimilar results were found for transfusion needs.\n\nWhere appropriate, dose-response effects were estimated using logistic regression and 22 articles were identified.\n\nDexamethasone and risk of nausea and vomiting and postoperative bleeding after tonsillectomy in children: a randomized trial.\n\nCONTEXT Dexamethasone is widely used to prevent postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV) in pediatric tonsillectomy.   \n\n\nAcetaminophen-codeine and ibuprofen were given as postoperative analgesia.\n\nFollow-up continued until the 10th postoperative day.   \n\n\nChildren who received dexamethasone received significantly less ibuprofen.\n\nThere were 26 postoperative bleeding episodes in 22 children.\n\nTwo of 53 (4%; 95% CI, 0.5%-13%)\n\nEight children had to undergo emergency reoperation because of bleeding, all of whom had received dexamethasone.\n\nThe trial was stopped early for safety reasons.   \n\n\nTRIAL REGISTRATION clinicaltrials.gov Identifier: NCT00403806.\n\nINTERVENTION Universal prestorage leukoreduction program introduced by 2 Canadian blood agencies.\n\nSecondary outcomes included rates of posttransfusion fever and antibiotic use.   \n\n\nDexamethasone decreases vomiting by children after tonsillectomy.\n\nAnesthesia was maintained with N2O and halothane.\n\nDexamethasone 150 micrograms/kg up to a maximum dose of 8 mg, or placebo, was administered IV before surgery.\n\nAll patients received 1.5 mg/kg codeine intramuscularly (IM) intraoperatively.\n\nPerioperative IV fluids, management of emesis, postoperative pain and hospital discharge criteria were all standardized.\n\nVomiting, both in-hospital and postdischarge, was decreased by the prophylactic administration of dexamethasone.\n\nEach episode of in-hospital vomiting prolonged discharge by 13 +/-\n\n2 min, mean +/-\n\nSD (P < 0.001).\n\nIn conclusion, dexamethasone markedly decreased vomiting by healthy children after elective tonsillectomy in an ambulatory hospital setting.\n\nEffect of hydroxyethyl starch on bleeding after cardiopulmonary bypass: a meta-analysis of randomized trials.\n\nOBJECTIVE The effects of hydroxyethyl starch on bleeding after cardiopulmonary bypass were determined.   \n\n\nRandomized trials directly comparing different hydroxyethyl starch solutions were also included.   \n\n\nRESULTS Eighteen trials with 970 total patients were included.\n\nAlbumin improved hemodynamics.\n\nThere were no differences in fluid balance, ventilator time, intensive care unit stay, or mortality.   \n\n\nThere was no evidence that these risks could be mitigated by lower molecular weight and substitution.\n\nDose-dependent effect of statins on the incidence of acute kidney injury after cardiac surgery.\n\nAfter full adjustment for all independent predictors of AKI, the results did not change.",
